This directory is intentionally empty, and intentionally tracked.

`kernel/build.rs` and `kernel/ada/regen-prebuilt.py` both compile the Ada
sources with `--RTS=kernel/ada/rts`. GNAT validates that path before reading
any Ada, and rejects it outright if there is no `adalib/` beside `adainclude/`:

    gnat1: RTS path not valid: missing adalib directory

We link no Ada runtime -- `adainclude/system.ads` is a stub and there are no
library objects -- so `adalib/` has nothing to hold. But it must exist, and git
cannot track an empty directory, which is why this file is here.

Deleting it is not cosmetic. Without the directory the compile fails, and the
failure lands in build.rs's warning arm, which downgrades to a cargo:warning and
carries on. The build still succeeds; what is silently lost is the check that
`kernel/ada/prebuilt/virtqueue_descriptors.o` is byte-for-byte what the compiler
actually produces. The source stamp does not cover that -- it records what the
object should have been built from, never what it contains -- so a tampered or
mis-generated object would link with nothing to catch it.

That is not hypothetical: the directory was absent from the repository from the
day the check was written until 2026-09-05, so the verification had never once
run on any machine.
